From 84fd5591c40e97d868958c6a0222bd46c520fe77 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 19:21:26 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0081 -- scene-construct/wait-next-frame
 timing, closing round

The 8-bit optimization pass (Trilby's Notes, TNP80A/TNP80B, reconciled
against the trusted 1000-tick TN8A1/TN8A2 baseline after a short-window
methodological error was caught and corrected) found that patches
0079/0080's four instrumented phases together account for only ~17% of
the corrected 29047ms steady-state window (load time already
subtracted out) -- 83% still unaccounted for. Three remaining
candidates, all in the same per-tick loop:

- scene_construct: ac/draw.cpp's construct_game_scene() +
  construct_game_screen_overlay() -- the draw-LIST-building step
  (walks room/UI objects, appends to gfxDriver's draw list), confirmed
  to be a distinct, earlier, non-recursive phase from
  RenderToBackBuffer()'s own sprite blitting by reading render_graphics()
  directly: it calls these two, then gfxDriver->Render() (which calls
  RenderToBackBuffer(), already measured by patch 0079).
- flip/present cost: not new code -- patch 0035's existing
  DOSAGS_FLIP_INSTR hook, just enabled alongside this round's real-
  hardware cell instead of left off.
- wait_next_frame: ac/timer.cpp's WaitForNextFrame() -- read carefully
  before instrumenting, per the operator's explicit caution (this
  project's own dos-hardware-validation precedent: a busy-wait once
  starved the BIOS timer and made a self-reported fps number go UP
  while real performance collapsed). Confirmed this implementation
  uses a real std::this_thread::sleep_for() (not a spin/poll loop) and
  a "jump ahead if lagging" clamp (MAXIMUM_FALL_BEHIND=3 frames) that
  should make it request near-zero sleep for a game already running
  below its target frame rate -- but rather than trust that reasoning
  alone, this patch tracks BOTH the function's own measured wall-clock
  cost (wait_next_frame) AND, separately, what it itself computed as
  the requested sleep duration whenever positive
  (wait_next_frame_sleep_calls/wait_next_frame_requested_ms, tracked
  at the actual sleep_for() call site) -- so a real run's data can show
  whether measured cost tracks requested sleep (expected pacing) or
  vastly exceeds it (a miscalibrated wait, the exact failure mode a
  bare wall-clock number cannot distinguish from correct behavior on
  its own).

Extends patch 0079/0080's own struct/macros. New DOS-PORT tickstats
lines in quit.cpp's report, same full-run running-aggregate shape.

